And I feel like that's what's actually called for at this point is just honest labeling like like you make a nice show people are moved by it i was moved by it and if it were labeled honestly i think everybody would react differently to it i don't think that label covers the totality of what it is the label fiction yeah we have different world views on some of these things i agree i know but i feel like but i feel like i have the normal worldview like the normal worldview is somebody stands on a stage and says this happened to me i think it happened to them unless it's clearly labeled here's a work of fiction i really regret putting the show on this american life and it was wrong for me to misrepresent to you and to brian that it could be on the show mike daisy i wanted to say before we leave this subject that i and my co-workers at this american American life take our mistake

00:41:46.160 --> 00:41:48.580
in putting Mike's story onto the air very seriously.

00:41:49.640 --> 00:42:01.460
As I said earlier in the program, when Mike told us that it would be impossible for us to talk to his interpreter for fact-checking purposes, we should have killed the story right there and then, and to do anything else was a screw-up.

00:42:02.880 --> 00:42:04.260
This is an unusual situation for us.

00:42:04.340 --> 00:42:13.460
Generally, if we are working with a non-journalist on a story, one of our producers is actually there for every step of the tape gathering and the reporting so we know what is true.

00:42:14.450 --> 00:42:19.560
And when we do our own reporting, we subject it to the same standards as other reporting that you hear on public radio.

00:42:20.440 --> 00:42:28.800
I was a reporter and a producer for the big daily news shows before I started this program, and we follow the same rules of reporting here that I followed there.

00:42:29.220 --> 00:42:30.980
We vet and we check our stories.

00:42:31.020 --> 00:42:35.420
And when we present something to you as true, it's because we believe in its factual accuracy.

00:42:37.480 --> 00:42:42.860
Which brings us to Act 3. Act 3, the news that's fit to print.

00:42:43.880 --> 00:42:55.700
So to end today's program, all of us here at our show, we wanted to review one more time, what exactly do we know about working conditions for the people who make iPhones and MacBooks and other Apple products in China?

00:42:56.640 --> 00:42:59.760
And to answer that question, we turn to New York Times reporter Charles Duhigg.

00:43:00.260 --> 00:43:16.240
In January, he and Times correspondent David Barbosa wrote the the newspaper's front page investigative series about this very subject, Duhigg says that a lot of what we know about the conditions for Apple workers in China comes from Apple itself, which issues a report each year on this.

00:43:16.760 --> 00:43:26.520
In addition, there's a number of organizations in China that are either advocacy organizations or sort of watchdog organizations that have also gone into factories and have published reports.

00:43:27.140 --> 00:43:31.080
And so I can kind of walk through what we know and precisely how we know it.

00:43:31.120 --> 00:43:31.420
Great.

00:43:32.140 --> 00:43:35.680
So in 2005, Apple created what was called the Supplier Code of Conduct.

00:43:36.340 --> 00:43:41.820
And the Supplier Code of Conduct said, these are the standards that we expect anyone who's making an Apple product to abide by.

00:43:42.880 --> 00:43:52.500
One of those, and in fact, the one that's probably most violated, is that they said that no one should work more than 60 hours per week that's working inside a factory that's making an Apple product.

00:43:53.760 --> 00:44:03.940
We know from Apple's own audits and the reports that they have published that at least 50% of all audited factories every year since 2007 have violated at least that provision.

00:44:04.840 --> 00:44:09.320
More than half of the workers whose records are examined are working more than 60 hours per week.

00:44:09.500 --> 00:44:10.620
Now, is that necessarily so bad?

00:44:10.820 --> 00:44:15.420
I mean, aren't a lot of these workers moving to the city to work as many hours as possible?

00:44:15.500 --> 00:44:18.880
They're away from their families, they're young, and they're there to make money, and they don't care.

00:44:19.380 --> 00:44:20.020
That's exactly right.

00:44:20.180 --> 00:44:27.320
You know, when we talked, my colleague David Barbosa, as well as a number of translators, have spoken I've spoken to a number of employees in these factories and that's exactly what they say.

00:44:28.060 --> 00:44:29.120
And Apple says that as well.

00:44:29.240 --> 00:44:38.300
They say, look, one of the reasons why there is so much overtime that's inappropriate and in some places is illegal is because the workers themselves are demanding that overtime.

00:44:39.300 --> 00:44:41.120
Now workers don't always say that.

00:44:41.260 --> 00:44:44.960
What workers often say is that they feel coerced into doing overtime.

00:44:45.440 --> 00:44:52.440
That if they didn't do overtime when it's asked of them, that they wouldn't get any overtime at all and that financially they would suffer as a result.

00:44:52.940 --> 00:44:56.300
So there are two stories here about how much people have to work.

00:44:56.380 --> 00:45:04.880
And there's a number of people that we have spoken to, the New York Times has spoken to, who have told us, for instance, that they had to do two 12-hour shifts in a row.

00:45:05.040 --> 00:45:07.100
So they're effectively working almost a full day.

00:45:07.160 --> 00:45:08.320
They're called continuous shifts.

00:45:10.600 --> 00:45:16.020
So I think when we talk about the conditions inside the plants where Apple products are made, we can sort of put them into two buckets.

00:45:16.740 --> 00:45:22.080
There's basically harsh work conditions, people being asked to work shifts that are too long.

00:45:22.520 --> 00:45:38.760
People being asked to stand or sit in backless chairs, people being asked to work in plants that are still under construction, or people living in dorms that are provided by the companies, Foxconn and others, where they say that those conditions, the living conditions are harsh.

00:45:40.040 --> 00:45:47.720
Workers have told us that they live in dorm rooms where there's anywhere from 12 to sometimes 20 or 30 people stuffed into a single apartment.

00:45:48.220 --> 00:45:51.140
So it's very, very crowded, very, very unpleasant conditions.

00:45:53.180 --> 00:45:54.820
that's the first bucket of issues.

00:45:55.740 --> 00:45:58.920
And those are all kind of, we wouldn't like to work there.

00:45:58.960 --> 00:45:59.740
It sounds really unpleasant.

00:46:00.920 --> 00:46:09.480
I do not think that you would find any factory in America where you would find those same conditions, and you would not find any Americans who would tolerate those conditions.

00:46:10.500 --> 00:46:18.680
That being said, I think that China is a little bit different, and that the expectations, particularly as a developing nation, of workers are a little bit different.

00:46:19.320 --> 00:46:25.020
I don't think I think holding them to American standards is precisely the right way to look at this situation.

00:46:26.180 --> 00:46:32.600
The second bucket, which is much smaller, is actually safety and life-threatening issues.

00:46:32.940 --> 00:46:38.980
And what we know about those conditions are isolated incidents that either injured or claimed lives.

00:46:39.390 --> 00:46:51.580
So one of the best examples of this was last year, within a seven-month period, there were two explosions inside factories where iPads were being produced that killed four people and injured 77 others.

00:46:52.240 --> 00:47:01.740
Both of those explosions were caused by dust that's created through the process of polishing the aluminum that makes up the case of an iPad.

00:47:01.780 --> 00:47:35.680
had prior to those explosions there was a report released by this group saccom or students and scholars against corporate misbehavior an advocacy group an advocacy group warning about safety conditions within at least one of the plants and saying there's dust here and dust is a known safety hazard in all kinds of plants in all kinds of plants right all types of dust you have to remove it or else it can explode saccom had sent a report base saccom says to apple and to foxconn weeks before this explosion occurred, saying things need to be changed.

00:47:36.460 --> 00:47:52.360
The explosion that occurred in a city named Chengdu that killed four people preceded by a number of months a second explosion that happened in Shanghai at a completely different plant in a completely different factory, but that has the same root cause.

00:47:53.080 --> 00:48:11.280
And so what people, critics of Apple have said is, if Apple had taken this first explosion seriously enough, They could have gone in and they could have required every company, every plant where aluminum polishing was occurring to improve conditions, and they could have prevented or averted the second explosion.

00:48:11.700 --> 00:48:16.500
Yeah, you write in your article, you point out that the second explosion happened seven months after the first one.

00:48:16.540 --> 00:48:24.740
And you quote a man named Nicholas Ashford, who's a former chairman of the National Advisory Committee on Occupational Safety and Health, which advises the U.S.

00:48:24.800 --> 00:48:25.260
Department of Labor.

00:48:26.040 --> 00:48:31.220
He said, it's gross negligence after an explosion occurs not to realize that every factory should be inspected.

00:48:31.780 --> 00:48:35.020
He said, if it were terribly difficult to deal with aluminum dust, I would understand.

00:48:35.900 --> 00:48:37.730
But do you know how easy dust is to control?

00:48:37.940 --> 00:48:38.940
It's called ventilation.

00:48:39.180 --> 00:48:41.160
We solved this problem over a century ago.

00:48:42.080 --> 00:48:42.960
That's exactly right.

00:48:43.080 --> 00:48:45.480
That was what Mr. Ashford had told us.

00:48:46.480 --> 00:48:52.390
But again, these two buckets, I mean, I think the important thing here is that some of these are simply very, very harsh conditions.

00:48:52.580 --> 00:48:54.580
And some of these are life-threatening situations.

00:48:56.120 --> 00:49:02.540
and the life-threatening conditions, as far as we know, seem to be limited to a relatively small number of incidents.

00:49:04.440 --> 00:49:17.240
In the investigative series that Duhigg did with David Barbosa for The Times, they note that in Apple's own reports, year after year, Apple finds that violations of its own labor standards continue in its Chinese plans.

00:49:18.260 --> 00:49:23.400
Last year, there were some slight improvements, but these go up and down, and the problems include some very serious ones.

00:49:23.860 --> 00:49:37.120
And in their series, they quote an unnamed former Apple executive who has firsthand knowledge of all this is saying, quote, if you see the same pattern of problems year after year, that means the company is ignoring the issue rather than solving it.

00:49:37.420 --> 00:49:40.480
If we meant business, core violations would disappear.

00:49:41.500 --> 00:49:46.700
Then when I asked Charles Duhigg about this, he says that not everyone at Apple sees it that way.

00:49:47.220 --> 00:49:49.740
When I talk to Apple sources, they sort of respond to that in two ways.

00:49:49.940 --> 00:49:54.860
First of all, they say, we feel as a company, we are limited in how many changes we can make.

00:49:55.120 --> 00:49:56.980
We can only push our suppliers so far.

00:49:57.900 --> 00:50:02.400
Others from within Apple, former Apple executives say, that's a self-imposed limitation.

00:50:03.340 --> 00:50:09.820
If Apple demanded X and said, we're willing to fire you if we don't get X, then X would happen immediately.

00:50:10.900 --> 00:50:15.980
One of the things that you and David Barboza write about in your series is you write about the tight profit margins for Apple suppliers.

00:50:16.020 --> 00:50:20.000
Could you just explain how that works and how that factors into this?

00:50:20.220 --> 00:50:22.260
Absolutely, because that has a huge impact on this.

00:50:23.060 --> 00:50:32.320
So Apple is known within the supplier's supply chain as being one of the most aggressive negotiators in terms of the prices that they're willing to pay.

00:50:32.740 --> 00:50:38.480
Because everyone knows that if you land Apple as a client, it helps your reputation enormously.

00:50:38.820 --> 00:50:44.620
So essentially every supplier out there wants to work with Apple because it's like a badge that they can bring.

00:50:45.220 --> 00:50:46.140
They can bring the quality.

00:50:46.240 --> 00:50:47.020
They can bring the volume.

00:50:47.220 --> 00:50:47.560
Exactly.

00:50:48.040 --> 00:50:48.780
Apple is the gold standard.

00:50:50.160 --> 00:51:10.120
As a result, Apple has this enormous negotiating power, and they use it, I am told by our sources, very aggressively to come in and basically say, show us your entire cost structure, every single part of what you pay and piece of your internal economics, and we are going to give you a razor-thin profit margin that you're allowed to keep.

00:51:10.400 --> 00:51:23.060
Now, a number of companies and a number of activists outside of companies and other companies This is part of the reason why conditions are so harsh among Apple suppliers, is because they literally don't have the money to pay for better conditions.

00:51:24.020 --> 00:51:35.230
That once Apple comes in and says, we're going to give you a razor-thin profit margin, that's when companies start cutting corners, or they can't afford to hire more people in order to work on the line so that you don't have to work these long stretches.

00:51:35.320 --> 00:51:35.420
searches.

00:51:36.400 --> 00:51:48.640
One of the most interesting things and one of the newest things that I think you pointed out in this series is that the cost of labor in an iPhone, if it were made in the United States, would be only about $65 more per phone.

00:51:49.020 --> 00:51:51.080
I mean, that's a lot of money if you're manufacturing stuff.

00:51:52.060 --> 00:52:05.620
But with iPhones selling with hundreds of dollars profit in each phone, Apple could still make a profit if it were manufacturing in the US. And you have an entire article where you lay out That is not actually the main reason why these are made overseas at this point.

00:52:06.140 --> 00:52:06.620
That's exactly right.

00:52:06.700 --> 00:52:08.780
And that $65, that's the high-end estimate.

00:52:08.920 --> 00:52:16.240
Some people told us that you could, from a labor perspective, you could build the iPhone in the United States for just $10 extra a phone if you're paying American wages.

00:52:16.980 --> 00:52:21.900
But wages, labor is such an enormously small part of any electronic device, right?

00:52:21.900 --> 00:52:35.440
Compared to the cost of buying chips or making sure that you have a plant that can turn out thousands of these things a day or being able to get strengthened glass cut exactly right within two days of this thing being due, that's what's important.

00:52:35.600 --> 00:52:37.580
Labor is almost insignificant.

00:52:38.740 --> 00:52:42.640
What is really important are supply chains and flexibility of factories.

00:52:43.560 --> 00:52:54.120
You want to be able to be located right next to the plant that makes the screws so that that when you need a small change to that screw factory, you can go next door and say, give it to me in six hours.

00:52:54.280 --> 00:52:55.440
And they can say, here you go.

00:52:56.000 --> 00:52:59.610
Because if that factory was in another state or on another continent, it would take two weeks.

00:53:00.020 --> 00:53:02.960
It's the flexibility within the Chinese manufacturing system.

00:53:03.960 --> 00:53:06.320
That's what you can do in Asia that you can't do in the United States.

00:53:06.520 --> 00:53:08.640
There's a bunch of incredible stories you tell in that article.

00:53:08.720 --> 00:53:14.970
And one of them is you talk about the number of industrial engineers needed to oversee 200,000 assembly assembly line workers.

00:53:15.300 --> 00:53:18.940
You say there's 8,700 industrial engineers that you need.

00:53:19.120 --> 00:53:29.100
And so to get this plant going, to get this particular operation going that you're writing about, I can't remember which one it is, you said it would take nine months to find those 8,700 industrial engineers in the United States.

00:53:29.420 --> 00:53:31.040
And in China, how long it took?

00:53:31.540 --> 00:53:31.980
15 days.

00:53:33.060 --> 00:53:40.700
And that 15-day figure, the guy who told me that also told me that that's basically because they kind of drug their heels on it a little bit.

00:53:40.820 --> 00:53:42.440
They probably could have done it faster.

00:53:43.860 --> 00:54:15.220
to get to the normative question that's kind of underlying all the reporting and all the discussion of this the thing that we all want to know when we hear this is like wait should i feel bad about this and and i you know i mean as somebody who owns these products should i feel bad and i don't know that i feel so bad when when i hear this so it's not my job to tell you whether you should feel bad or not right i'm a reporter for the new york times my job is to find facts and essentially let you make a decision on your own let me let me pose the argument that people people have posed to me about why you should feel bad and you can make of it what you will.

00:54:16.400 --> 00:54:25.540
And that argument is there were times in this nation when we had harsh working conditions as part of our economic development.

00:54:27.020 --> 00:54:28.960
We decided as a nation that that was unacceptable.

00:54:30.000 --> 00:54:48.420
We passed laws in order to prevent those harsh working conditions from ever being inflicted on American workers Again, and what has happened today is that rather than exporting that standard of life, which is within our capacity to do, we have exported harsh working conditions to another nation.

00:54:49.040 --> 00:55:00.040
So should you feel bad that someone is working 12 to 24 hours a day in order to produce the iPhone that you know that when you say it like that, suddenly I feel bad again.

00:55:00.380 --> 00:55:02.900
I don't know whether you should feel bad, right?

00:55:02.900 --> 00:55:03.920
I mean, finish your thought.

00:55:04.880 --> 00:55:06.080
Should you feel bad about that?

00:55:06.160 --> 00:55:07.500
I don't know.

00:55:07.540 --> 00:55:08.280
That's for you to judge.

00:55:08.900 --> 00:55:27.300
But I think the way to pose that question is, do you feel comfortable knowing that iPhones and iPads and other products could be manufactured in less harsh conditions, but that these harsh conditions exist because of an economy that you are supporting with your dollars?

00:55:27.300 --> 00:55:29.340
I am the direct beneficiary of those harsh conditions.

00:55:29.900 --> 00:55:30.850
You're not only the direct beneficiary.

00:55:31.860 --> 00:55:33.740
You are actually one of the reasons why it exists.

00:55:35.180 --> 00:55:48.840
If you made different choices, if you demanded different conditions, if you demanded that other people enjoy the same work protections that you yourself enjoy, then those conditions would be different overseas.

00:55:50.360 --> 00:55:50.800
Charles Duhigg.

00:55:51.260 --> 00:55:55.740
You can find the series that he did with David Barboza about Apple and China at the New York Times website.

